From: vuln-patch <patch@chainguard.dev>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix CGP-q285-qppx-f8fx: bind CSRF field name as GPath
 variable

FormAuthFilter's CSRF auto-detection (CWE-94, code injection; also
CWE-116 improper output encoding and CWE-74 injection) let a
server-controlled HTML hidden-input name attribute reach a Groovy
GroovyShell.evaluate() call as part of the compiled expression's
source text. In FormAuthFilter.filter(), the extracted csrfFieldName
was interpolated with String.format into the FIND_INPUT_FIELD_WITH_NAME
GPath template ("html.depthFirst().grep { ... it.@name == '%s' }
.collect { it.@value }.get(0)") and the resulting string was passed to
html.getString(...), which XMLAssertion.eval() compiles and executes
with an unrestricted GroovyShell. A crafted hidden-input name such as
"' }; Runtime.getRuntime().exec(...); x={ '" broke out of the quoted
string literal and ran arbitrary Groovy/Java code with the full
privileges of the JVM process running the test suite, reachable both
through the documented isAutoDetectCsrfFieldName() path and through
the explicit FormAuthConfig.withCsrfFieldName(...) path, since both
funneled into the same interpolation.

The root cause was building the evaluated expression's source text by
concatenating untrusted, response-derived data into it. The fix
removes that concatenation entirely instead of trying to escape it:
FIND_INPUT_FIELD_WITH_NAME is now a fixed, compile-time constant that
references an unquoted Groovy identifier (restAssuredCsrfFieldName),
and the untrusted csrfFieldName value is bound as a runtime variable
via XmlPath's own param(name, value) mechanism
(html.param(CSRF_FIELD_NAME_PARAM, csrfFieldName).getString(
FIND_INPUT_FIELD_WITH_NAME)), which XMLAssertion.eval() passes into
GroovyShell's Binding. A bound variable is pure data to the Groovy
compiler and can never alter the parsed structure of the expression,
so no character sequence, escape convention, or encoding (including
Unicode-escape forms such as \u0027 that a naive character escaper
would miss) can smuggle a breakout. This guards the single sink line
in FormAuthFilter.groovy that both the auto-detect and the explicit
withCsrfFieldName branches reach, without touching the general-purpose
XMLAssertion.eval()/JSONAssertion GroovyShell evaluation engine that
the library and its callers rely on for ordinary, developer-authored
GPath/JsonPath expressions.

The regression test added is
FormAuthCsrfGroovyInjectionExploitTest, which drives the vulnerability
end to end through the public
RestAssured.given().auth().form(user, pass, formAuthConfig) entry
point against a local HTTP server serving a crafted login page. It
asserts that no OS command executes for: the documented exec-based
breakout via auto-detection, the same breakout supplied through
withCsrfFieldName(...), a \u0027 Unicode-escape smuggling variant, and
a String.execute() variant, while also asserting that legitimate CSRF
field names containing underscore, hyphen, period, and colon are
still located and their value sent correctly through both extraction
paths.

CGP: CGP-q285-qppx-f8fx
